WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.360
I think the last two days

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:09.120
have been very challenging for me
because after winning against Rafa,

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.280
I almost felt
like the tournament was done

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.720
because most of the times
when we meet,

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.480
it's in the finals of events.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.800
This time, it was in the semi-final

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.840
and it was a great battle
lasting over four hours.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:23.680
I needed to regroup.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.720
I needed to recharge my batteries

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.720
and try to come out on the court
against Tsitsipas,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.000
who was in form,
and perform my best.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.760
It wasn't easy at all...

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:33.920
considering what I've been through
in the semi-finals.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.640
considering what I've been through
in the semi-finals.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.960
It took a lot out of me emotionally,

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.720
physically and mentally as well.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.200
After a bit of a slower start
for me,

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.480
I found my groove,
I found my rhythm

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.360
and adjusted to the conditions that
were different from the semi-finals.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.320
And after a very good start
in the third set,

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.000
there was no looking back.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.040
I found my game

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.600
and I managed
to start to read his game

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.280
and finish off the match
in the best possible style.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.400
Coming back from two sets down,

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.920
I don't think I've ever done that
in the finals of a slam.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.160
So, it makes this win extra special.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.560
Well, Paris has been

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.800
a very particular place for me
in my career

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.760
because I've tried so many times
to win Roland-Garros

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.840
and it was the only Grand Slam
that I never won

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.240
and that I was missing until 2016.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.120
I can recall the feelings
that I had on the court

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.680
when I won that match in 2016.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.240
It was, I think,
most of all, a huge relief

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.080
because there was
so much expectations and pressure

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.200
being built from myself

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.920
and then, of course,
from everyone else around me,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.120
that I had to win it,

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.000
after losing two or three finals.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.480
And ever since then...

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.600
I was always
kind of coming back to Paris

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.400
hoping that I could do it again,

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.800
at least before I ended my career.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.000
It's not like my end
is anywhere near.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:07.840
I'm not thinking about it,
but I just felt like

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.120
winning Roland-Garros for me
was probably the biggest task

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.560
of any other Slam,

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.240
because of Rafa
and just because of the surface,

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.120
and I think the game itself.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:22.880
So, to win it again at this age
and at this stage of my career

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.240
is something very remarkable.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.720
I'm very grateful and very thrilled
with that achievement.

